      "target": "../sections/page-002.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "SWOT analysis\nTanya Sammut-Bonnici and David Galea\nCONCEPTUAL FRAMEWORK\nA\nSWOT\nanalysis\nevaluates\nthe\ninternal\nstrengths and weaknesses, and the external\nopportunities and threats in an organization’s\nenvironment. The internal analysis is used to\nidentify resources, capabilities, core competen-\ncies, and competitive advantages inherent to the\norganization. The external analysis identifies\nmarket opportunities and threats by looking\nat competitors’ resources, the industry envi-\nronment, and the general environment (see\nFigure 1). The objective of a SWOT analysis is\nto use the knowledge an organ",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "First page of the Wiley Encyclopedia of Management entry \"SWOT analysis\" by Sammut-Bonnici and Galea. A SWOT analysis assesses internal strengths and weaknesses and external opportunities and threats so that an organisation can formulate its strategy; the article offers templates and practical guidance. Internal analysis identifies the source of competitive advantage: resources (tangible, such as raw materials and machinery, or intangible, such as finance, technology, human capital, patents and brand equity) combine into capabilities, which in turn yield core competencies that, if unique, give sustainable advantage and are hard for rivals to imitate. The internal resources fall into functional categories (financial, managerial, infrastructural, suppliers, manufacturing, distribution, marketing, innovation). Financial resources are access to capital, which is cheaper for established, reputable firms; managerial resources are illustrated by football clubs moving from star players to dynamic teams.",
