      "narrative": null,
      "content": "2\nSWOT analysis\nSWOT\nanalysis\n \nAnalysis of\n \ninternal environment\nStrengths and Weaknesses\n(micro environment)\nOrganizational functions\nAnalysis of \nexternal envrionment\nOpportunities and Threats\n(macro environment)\nCompetitor environment\nIndustry environment\nGeneral environment\nFigure 1 SWOT analysis main components.\nInternal environment \nStrengths \nWeaknesses \naction\n1. Financial\n2. Managerial\n3. Infrastructural\n4. Suppliers\n5. Manufacturing\n6. Distribution channels\n7. Marketing\n8. Brand equity\n9. Innovation resources\nStrategic\nSWOT analysis template\nInternal Strengths (S) and Weaknesses (W",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Figures 1 and 2 and the continuation of the internal analysis. Figure 1 divides SWOT analysis into the internal environment (strengths and weaknesses, organisational functions) and the external environment (opportunities and threats in the competitor, industry and general environments). Figure 2 is a template listing nine internal factors (financial, managerial, infrastructural, suppliers, manufacturing, distribution channels, marketing, brand equity, innovation resources) against strengths, weaknesses and strategic action. The text continues with infrastructural resources, the computerised systems for finance, procurement, production, logistics and customer relationships and the platforms they run on, and begins on suppliers.",
