      "target": "../sections/page-004.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "SWOT analysis\n3\nadvantage of an organization. Different methods\nand techniques are used to rate and evaluate\nsuppliers. An effective assessment is based\non the key deliverables of the product range,\nquality, availability, lead times, and service\nresponsiveness.\nManufacturing\nresources\nsuch\nas\nplant,\nmachinery, automation, and technical support\nare essential to develop quality products. Flex-\nibility in manufacturing is another factor to\nconsider as it enables innovation and product\ndevelopment.\nModular\nmanufacturing\nand\noutsourcing of production are other factors that\nwould cater for innovatio",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "The remaining internal factors, resource similarity and the challenges of internal analysis. Suppliers are rated on range, quality, availability, lead times and responsiveness; manufacturing on plant, automation and flexibility; distribution on logistics and channel partners; marketing on research, product, pricing, channel and promotion; brand equity on reputation with stakeholders; and innovation on culture plus copyrights, patents and trademarks. When competitors have very similar resources, as mobile operators such as Vodafone and T-Mobile share network and handset suppliers, they tend to adopt similar strategies and engage in coopetition, growing the market together. A common mistake in internal analysis is listing every factor in which the firm is thought strong or weak, which is hard to turn into strategic action.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
