      "target": "../sections/page-005.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "4\nSWOT analysis\nbearing on the ultimate source of competitive\nadvantage that an organization is seeking to\nachieve. For example, having high levels of\ncleanliness and hygiene in a back office oper-\nation is intrinsically beneficial but unlikely\nto make a significant contribution toward the\nsource of competitive advantage that the orga-\nnization is seeking. On the other hand, high\nlevels of cleanliness and hygiene are compulsory\nin industries such as catering establishments,\npharmaceutical manufacturers, and hospital\ninstitutions – where client health and safety\ncould be compromised. In these c",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "End of the internal-analysis challenges and the start of external analysis. Practitioners should keep only factors that bear on the intended source of competitive advantage, judged against competitors; hygiene, for instance, is a basic requirement rather than a strength in catering, pharmaceuticals or hospitals. External analysis covers variables beyond the organisation's control. The examples given are an opportunity, mobile-money transactions estimated to reach $1 trillion by 2015, and a threat, online reading overtaking print, which pushes publishers such as Wiley and Pearson toward mobile formats. External factors fall into three areas (Figure 3): the competitor environment (rivals' resources and capabilities, illustrated by Nokia's challengers), the industry environment (Porter's five forces), and the general environment (PEST and its variants).",
