      "target": "../sections/page-006.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "SWOT analysis\n5\nSWOT analysis template\nExternal Opportunities (O) and Threats (T)\n(macro analysis)\nExternal environments\nOpportunities \nThreats\nStrategic\naction\nCompetitor environment\nFinancial\nManagerial\nOrganizational\nSuppliers\nManufacturing\nDistribution channels\nMarketing\nBrand equity\nInnovation resources\nIndustry environment\nIntensity of rivalry among competitors\nThreat of new entrants\nThreat of substitute products\nBargaining power of suppliers\nBargaining power of buyers\nGeneral environment\nPolitical\nEconomic\nSocio-cultural\nTechnological\nLegal\nEnvironmental\nDemographic\nEthical\nRegulatory\nF",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Figure 3 and the implications of the industry environment. Figure 3 is a template of external factors against opportunities, threats and strategic action: the competitor environment (the same resource categories as the internal template), the industry environment (Porter's five forces: rivalry, new entrants, substitutes, supplier power, buyer power) and the general environment (political, economic, socio-cultural, technological, legal, environmental, demographic, ethical, regulatory). In the text, the five forces determine competitors' business models and profitability. From the industrial-organisation perspective a firm should choose the markets that offer the best opportunities, which matter more than its internal resources; from the resource-based view it should find a position where it can influence or shield itself from the five forces.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
