      "target": "../sections/page-007.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "6\nSWOT analysis\nOpportunities and threats\nImpact\nCompetitor activity\nEstablishment of a hypermarket chain\nwith an outlet in close proximity to the\nsupermarket.\nPotential loss of customers to the new\noutlet that may afford to charge lower\nprices due to economies of scale.\nSuppliers\nEstablishment of a large number of new\nsuppliers for fruits and vegetables.\nIncreased competition is likely to drive\ndown their price.\nGovernment\nHarsher HACCP requirements to ensure\noptimal food safety in establishments\nHigher compliance costs in an effort to\nmake changes to existing operations to\ncomply with new re",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Figure 4 and the general environment. Figure 4 is a worked example for a supermarket chain: a nearby hypermarket (risk of losing customers to lower prices), many new fruit and vegetable suppliers (lower prices), stricter HACCP rules (higher compliance costs), recession (lower demand or a shift to cheaper food), time-poor health-conscious families (demand for healthy ready meals), and online channels (wider market reach). The text argues that firms able to shape their industry, such as Microsoft, Apple with the iPod, iPhone and iPad, or the consortia behind GSM and 3G, earn above-average profits. It notes that SWOT's external review leans toward the resource-based view. General-environment analysis uses PEST-type dimensions, and opportunities and threats are specific to a business, so new communication technology threatens traditional tour operators but opens the market to new intermediaries. Sources of external information begin to be listed.",
