      "pageEnd": 8,
      "target": "../sections/page-008.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "SWOT analysis\n7\nstructured research with suppliers, customers,\npotential customers, and the public. Employees\nand industry personnel who are in direct contact\nwith the market have a great deal of information\nabout the competitive landscape, competitive\nbehavior, and possible trends. Customer care\nrepresentatives, salespeople, procurement exec-\nutives, public relations, and communications\nagencies interact with the external environment\nand are good sources of information.\nCOGNITIVE INERTIA AND EXTERNAL\nANALYSIS\nAn organization’s environmental analysis can\nbe biased in terms of the level of focu",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Sources of external information, cognitive inertia and the TOWS matrix, with Figure 5. Information comes from trade press, research reports and trade shows, and from staff who deal with the market, such as sales, customer care and procurement. Strategists' cognitive maps, shaped by experience, can ignore market signals in fast-changing industries, and this cognitive inertia can lead to decline unless it is regularly re-examined. Weihrich's TOWS matrix (Figure 5) derives tactical strategies from four pairings: WO (use opportunities to overcome weaknesses, for example a joint venture or subcontracting to obtain screen technology for tablets), SO (use strengths to exploit opportunities), ST (use strengths against threats) and WT (minimise weaknesses and avoid threats).",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
