      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Page 9",
      "pageStart": 9,
      "pageEnd": 9,
      "target": "../sections/page-009.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "8\nSWOT analysis\nmaximize on both strengths and opportunities.\nApple was in a similar situation with a strong\ncash balance of $140 billion reported in 2013,\nmore than twice the size of Facebook’s market\nvalue. Apple employs a strategy of using excess\ncapital to develop its supply chain. More manu-\nfacturers are contracted to meet demand. More\ncapital is invested in process improvement and\nlogistics management to increase efficiency and\ndecrease the time to market.\nThe ST strategy uses the organization’s\ninternal strengths that can counteract threats\nfrom competitors, the industry, and the great",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "The rest of the TOWS strategies, a critique of SWOT and the bibliography. SO is illustrated by Apple's $140 billion cash balance in 2013, used to build its supply chain; ST by Microsoft's €561 million EU fine in 2013 over browser choice (€2.2 billion in EU fines over ten years), showing that a dominant firm must counter threats with caution; WT by the large tobacco companies' pre-emptive strategies against regulation and lawsuits. The authors' critique is that SWOT describes the current position but does not prescribe strategic action, so analysis should go beyond lists to cause and effect; combining it with the Balanced Scorecard and Quality Function Deployment has been proposed. They conclude that SWOT remains a useful tool for reviewing competitive position. The bibliography has six entries (Hitt and colleagues; Ip and Koo; McGee and colleagues; Sammut-Bonnici and McGee; Sirmon and colleagues; Weihrich and Cannice).",
